We’re excited to share Forrester’s newly released Total Economic Impact™ (TEI) study of IntelliJ IDEA, which JetBrains commissioned to understand how IntelliJ IDEA has delivered value historically and how it continues to do so.
Forrester interviewed nine senior engineering and technology leaders whose organizations have between 400 and 4,000 IntelliJ IDEA seats. Based on those interviews, it built a composite organization, a global financial services company with 2,000 developers, using IntelliJ IDEA.
Over three years, the composite organization saw:
A 155% return on investment
A net present value of $18.4 million
$30.3 million in benefits against $11.9 million in costs (present value)
Payback in nine months
The study highlighted IntelliJ IDEA’s impact on code quality, productivity (especially review time), and its open approach to AI, which allows developers to switch between models as their needs change or the tools evolve.
Code quality is the largest benefit
All nine interviewees named improved code quality as the biggest benefit of IntelliJ IDEA. In the study, code quality covers code review, testing, remediation, debugging, and refactoring – all crucial areas of development supported by capabilities built on more than 20 years of IDE intelligence. At the composite organization, this work accounts for about 30% of developers’ time, and we expect that number will only continue to grow as agents take on more code generation. 
“For the code review experience itself […] using agents to assist with that, we are seeing about a 12% to 15% faster PR cycle time.”
– Lead Principal Engineer, Software
Forrester models a 15% reduction in PR cycle time in Year 1, 17.5% in Year 2, and 20% in Year 3, as developers make more use of automated inspections, real-time code analysis, integrated testing, and refactoring tools. This benefit has a risk-adjusted present value of $16.3 million, more than half of the study’s total.
